# Uptodown MCP auth - a template only. Nothing loads .env automatically: set these in your
# shell or in the MCP client's `environment` block (see README.md -> Authentication).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Recommended: session auth (works with Google, GitHub and email logins)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Capture a browser session once, then the server reuses it:
#   npm run login
# The default flow is browser-first: it reuses a working session, else uses your Chrome (opening
# it when needed) and captures the session as soon as you are signed in there. Chrome asks once
# per run to allow the debugging connection; one-time setup: enable remote debugging at
# chrome://inspect/#remote-debugging (Chrome 144+; it stays enabled). A dedicated helper browser
# window is only used when Chrome cannot be launched. --helper and --manual never reuse the
# saved session. The capture has no age limit; only its own cookie expiries matter. That writes
# .uptodown-session.json at the package root. Alternatives:
#   UPTODOWN_SESSION_COOKIE=name=value; name2=value2
#   UPTODOWN_SESSION_FILE=C:\path\to\.uptodown-session.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Alternative: password auth
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# UPTODOWN_EMAIL=you@example.com
# UPTODOWN_PASSWORD=your-password

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Options
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Auth mode: auto (default) | session | password
# auto = use a captured session when one exists, otherwise fall back to email/password.
# UPTODOWN_AUTH_MODE=auto

# Optional: API base URL override. Defaults to https://www.uptodown.dev
# UPTODOWN_BASE_URL=https://www.uptodown.dev

# Optional: confine local upload paths to one directory. Every tool that reads a local file
# (check_file_hash with filePath, upload_app_file, upload_screenshots, upload_feature_graphic,
# update_app_icon) rejects paths outside it before hashing or opening the file. Relative values
# resolve against the server's working directory. Unset: any local path is accepted (the server
# prints a one-line reminder on stderr at startup).
# UPTODOWN_FILE_ROOT=C:\uploads

# Login helper (npm run login):
# Browser-first: reuses a saved session, else opens the site in your Chrome (launching it when
# needed) and captures the session automatically once you are signed in. One-time setup: enable
# remote debugging at chrome://inspect/#remote-debugging (Chrome 144+; it stays enabled). A
# dedicated helper browser window (profile .uptodown-browser/) is used only when Chrome cannot
# be launched, and closes itself on capture.
# UPTODOWN_CHROME_EXECUTABLE=C:\Program Files\Google\Chrome\Application\chrome.exe   # Chrome to launch when not running
# UPTODOWN_CHROME_DATA_DIR=C:\Users\you\AppData\Local\Google\Chrome\User Data   # advanced: profile to read DevToolsActivePort from
# UPTODOWN_LOGIN_TIMEOUT_MS=300000          # how long the login flow waits for the sign-in
# UPTODOWN_SESSION_FILE=C:\path\to\.uptodown-session.json   # where the helper writes (client reads the same)

# Login helper, helper window only (npm run login -- --helper; --playwright is an alias of it):
# UPTODOWN_BROWSER_CHANNEL=chrome
# UPTODOWN_LOGIN_HEADLESS=1

# Login helper, manual cURL capture only (npm run login -- --manual):
# UPTODOWN_LOGIN_NO_BROWSER=1        # do not open a browser (assume the tab is already open)
